{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;294,303p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '45,230p'; nl -ba codex-r24.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;294,303p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '45,230p'; nl -ba codex-r24.md\"","aggregated_output":"    91\t## 2. Before writing code: design\n    92\t\n    93\tCode written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code and before any review; a blank cell\n    94\tmeans it is not ready:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day: every state against every event it can meet (success,\n    97\t   refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends, a later run on a later date,\n    98\t   configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of the same data, two scopes at once), including\n    99\t   the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every\n   100\t   read and write uses.\n   101\t2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   102\t   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   103\t   against every reader of its output.\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n   294\t- **A score is a lead; the traces are the measurement.** Every measurement has a score, and every time you read the traces yourself: the\n   295\t  raw answer beside the article for every missed helpful entry, and a sample of the extras and of the hits; historical gold keeps its original labels. The scorer\n   296\t  hard-codes no rule of the standard: it reads the gold file it is given, prints the gold's and the standard's sha256, and refuses a gold\n   297\t  made for another version; scores are compared only on the same gold. Do not write a new scorer to judge a probe: read the traces yourself (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   298\t  \"不要写评分器这个之前就出问题过，你自己读trace 来分析\"). Alex, 2026-10-29 (D244): \"我觉得这是最容易出错的问题；你非常容易写死，我们在改的过程中如果drift你会一直用久的标准来衡量，这边需要有打分，但是“一定”要你自己下去看trace这是必须，记起来\".\n   299\t- **The ruler counts only what it was built to count.** Before a quality claim, a reader who did not write the change also reads the\n   300\t  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n   301\t  (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子\").\n   302\t- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind a metric's largest\n   303\t  values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.\n    45\t  stops the sequence (round 22 found no legitimate non-reader change on this trajectory). The four answers old-2's own run refused are\n    46\t  given back once each for non-readers, as in the `neither` pass.\n    47\t- Settlement at published rates with cache writes at 1.25x; reservation from request size (D696).\n    48\t\n    49\t## 3. The $0 proofs before the send\n    50\t\n    51\t1. `v120-replay` (done): poc-v120 with each probe draw's own answers meets all 17 link jobs and ends equal to old-2.\n    52\t2. **Linked dry run per draw** (a fake judge answering readers with markers): committed links equal probe draw n's proposals; no link\n    53\t   job outside the probe; no circle correction; every non-reader judgement equal to old-2's by question and day in its request and its\n    54\t   substantive fields (rung, reasons, reference, implied outcome, cites); **freshness checked on the archive side as well as the\n    55\t   transport side** (round 23: a cache hit bypasses the transport): every reader judgement made on a day at or after its D_R has a call\n    56\t   id above the copied archive's top, and none before; **the upstream snapshot:** when each reader prompt is built, the harness records\n    57\t   the judgement id, date, reference and forecast of each question it reads (wrapping `store.current_state` as `judging.build_prompt`\n    58\t   calls it) and checks the rendered dependency block against that snapshot, never against the final ledger; the cases a same-day\n    59\t   upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer (old-2's v14 has ten judgement days with no v8 judgement that day) and a refused\n    60\t   upstream answer followed by its accepted retry are each exercised; the added days listed.\n    61\t3. **Control dry run per draw:** the retired rows listed and checked; only the retired readers reach the transport, on the right days;\n    62\t   every other judgement equal to old-2's by question and day.\n    63\t4. The stop paths and the latch race, on the window.\n    64\t5. Price from old-2's archived usage of the readers' days (round 22: 247 judgements, $0.872 a sequence) plus the dry runs' added days,\n    65\t   prompt growth from the dependency block, retries, and the live reservations, each shown apart.\n    66\t\n    67\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    68\t\n    69\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    70\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    71\tforce that day.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    74\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    75\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    76\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    77\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    78\t\n    79\t**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n    80\t(overlap or clear, N) or unresolved (U), each date keeping its own arm's class even where the other arm is unresolved. Linked-n's\n    81\tdefinite count lies between D_L and D_L + U_L; control-n's between D_C and D_C + U_C.\n    82\t- **Improved:** D_L + U_L < D_C (the least favourable completion still has fewer definite dates in the linked arm).\n    83\t- **Not improved:** D_L >= D_C + U_C (even the most favourable completion cannot give the linked arm fewer).\n    84\t- **Undecided:** otherwise (the unresolved dates could change the result).\n    85\t- **Testable:** D_C >= 2 (known definite dates of the control over all 73, not only dates readable in both arms).\n    86\t- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved: a coverage policy that deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable\n    87\t  dates per arm (82.2%); the bounds above decide whether those dates matter.\n    88\t- **Credit (round 24):** a date the linked arm resolves as not definite while the control is definite on that date is a **gain**; every\n    89\t  distinct linked answer (v9's or v14's) that supplies a gain needed for \"improved\" is read for July's rule B (arithmetic following from\n    90\t  stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported). A gain resting on a material failure keeps its actual\n    91\t  class in the report but earns no credit: for P it counts definite. A gain that rests on an unsupported CPU increment (H4) is shown\n    92\t  with the increment removed when it can be quantified; when its size is unresolved and it could decide P, the pair is undecided.\n    93\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n    94\t\n    95\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n    96\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n    97\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n    98\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm when the linked arm has more than 3 refusals above its control on matched reader-days.\n    99\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   100\t  3 extra is far above that base rate while one or two stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   101\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   102\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   103\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   104\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   105\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   106\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   107\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   108\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   109\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. Checked regardless of keywords: every reader reference whose figure differs from\n   110\t  that reader's previous reference in the sequence in **amount, scope or attribution** (round 24: an unchanged $393B can move from a\n   111\t  company figure to a segment consensus), and every reader answer on the seven fixed dates below; keyword hits elsewhere are read too.\n   112\t  Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error and its control's conclusion in force on that date (its fresh judgement, or the one\n   113\t  carried forward) does not have the same specific error.\n   114\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   115\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. An unresolved CPU overlap that supplies a date's apparent\n   116\t  reconciliation (without the CPU-attributable uplift the date would be definite) counts that date definite in P.\n   117\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   118\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   119\t  source-backed read whose class decides. A material failure in a linked v9 or v14 answer whose carried-forward dates are counted\n   120\t  overlap or clear, and whose reconciliation rests on the failed assumption, counts those dates definite in P; a linked sequence with\n   121\t  more material failures than its control, by at least 2, is harm.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   124\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   125\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   126\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   127\t\n   128\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; round 24):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run, its\n   129\tformat and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send. One row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n   130\tboth judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n   131\tthe FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n   132\tthe exact request text (dependency block marked), the read judgement each figure came through, and the dated source quotes. Worked\n   133\texample (old-1 09-08): Data Center \"$419 billion for FY2027\" against total revenue \"$414.3 billion ... supports roughly $414–421\n   134\tbillion\": low end 419 is not above high end 421, so **overlap**, though the parser took $414.3B and flagged it definite. Claude reads\n   135\tfirst; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n   136\toutcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n   137\tabout 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews, 168 H5 marks before adjudication;\n   138\testimated 6–10 hours of reading across the readers, apart from the run's three hours.\n   139\t\n   140\t**Reported beside, not gating:** each link's relation, direction and sign (the weak CPU link disclosed); self-made references\n   141\t(`reference_census.py`'s measures, the `unsourced` scan with the dependency block excluded); v14 linked against control; v21's implied\n   142\ttotal against v9 and v14; linked-only judgement days.\n   143\t\n   144\t## 5. Cost (estimate; the ceiling is Alex's)\n   145\t\n   146\tReader judgements from their links, at published rates from old-2's archived usage: $0.872 a sequence (247 judgements, round 22).\n   147\tLinked sequences: about 19 added judgement days each (round 22's count on the fixed schedule; the dry runs give the exact count) and\n   148\tthe dependency block's prompt growth (about 10%): about $1.05. Three linked and three controls: about $5.8, plus retries and refusals\n   149\t(about 5%) and the live reservations (one judge call per sequence at once). Priced exactly from the dry runs before the ceiling\n   150\tquestion.\n   151\t\n   152\t## 6. Time\n   153\t\n   154\tOne paid call at a time per sequence: about 270–300 calls; old-2's stored reader calls took a median 38.2 seconds (round 23), so about\n   155\t3 hours with replay and retries, the six sequences at once.\n   156\t\n   157\t## 7. Round 22 findings and where they are answered\n   158\t\n   159\t1. Fresh judgements by date, request families, latest available upstream conclusion, the stored refusals: §1 (fresh judgements), §2,\n   160\t   §3 items 2–3. 2. P on annual ranges, carry-forward, coverage, all three pairs, outcomes for every case, the parser validated on\n   161\t   flagged and unflagged stored answers: §4, `parser-validation.md`. 3. H4 on every post-link v14 answer; H3 traced outside the\n   162\t   dependency block; H5 with an effect on the verdict; per-link report; refusals with a denominator, stops apart: §4. 4. July's latch:\n   163\t   §1, §2, §6.\n   164\tRound 23: freshness on the archive side, admission bound to the judging day, the upstream snapshot at prompt construction with its three\n   165\tcases: §2, §3 item 2. Every distinct pair hand-classified, misparses recorded, carried-forward dates: §4. Paired rates on jointly\n   166\treadable dates of the fixed 73, unresolved dates unable to reverse, conversions to overlap reported apart: §4 P. H3 traced through the\n   167\tlink to the outside statement: §4 H3. H1 second refusal, H2 invalidates, H4 and H5 consequences in P, disagreement resolution,\n   168\tverdict precedence: §4. Time: §6.\n   169\tRound 24: three outcomes per pair from two-sided bounds over all 73 dates, the floor on known control dates, 60 stated as a policy: §4 P.\n   170\tProduct against infrastructure failure, matched reader-days, the H1 tolerance's rationale: §4 H1 and precedence. Credit only for gains\n   171\tthat pass rule B, the CPU increment shown removed or the pair undecided, H3 triggers on amount, scope or attribution against the\n   172\tcontrol's conclusion in force: §4. The worksheet, its worked example, adjudication for P, H3, H4 and H5, reading time: §4.\n     1\t**NOT READY.** Version 3 fixes the latest-upstream proof and makes the parser a triage tool. Three gaps remain in §4. Uncertainty can become a failure, a linked double refusal can become inconclusive, and a forecast outside the H5 sample can still supply the apparent improvement.\n     2\t\n     3\t### Blocking changes\n     4\t\n     5\t1. **Give P three outcomes per pair.** Suppose 60 dates are jointly readable, the control has five definite contradictions and the linked arm has zero. On the other 13 dates, the control is clear and the linked forecast is unresolved. Both improvement and no improvement are possible. The current rule returns **fail**. Bound the difference in definite days over all 73 dates in both directions, retaining known classes on dates where only one arm is readable. Call a pair improved when even the least favourable completion improves, failed when even the most favourable completion cannot improve, and inconclusive when the unresolved dates could change the result. State explicitly whether the two-day control floor uses jointly readable dates. [Primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:78) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:109)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Separate product failure from infrastructure failure.** H1 calls a linked double refusal harm, but verdict step 1 makes any stop inconclusive. Production returns `judgement_refused_twice` and stops the day, so both rules apply to the same event. Make a confirmed linked double refusal fail and a control double refusal invalidate the comparison. Reserve the infrastructure outcome for budget, transport, and harness stops. For the rate check, count attempted reader judgements, including terminal refusals, and use matched reader-days for the paired rate. Report propagation-only refusals separately so extra clean judgement days cannot dilute the comparison. [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Product refusal path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/round.py:83)\n     8\t\n     9\t3. **Make decisive gains traceable beyond the seven H5 dates.** Keep the seven-date sample for H5’s comparative threshold. Also review the arithmetic and sourcing of every distinct linked answer that supplies a gain needed to pass P. Otherwise, an unsupported `v9` forecast between sample dates can carry the verdict. Preserve the actual forecast class separately from its eligibility for credit. If an unsupported CPU increment can be quantified, show the comparison without it. If its size is unresolved and it could decide P, withhold the pass. For H3, trigger review when a claim’s **amount, scope, or attribution** changes. An unchanged $393B can shift from company consensus to segment consensus. Use the paired control’s accepted conclusion in force on that date when it has no fresh judgement, and compare the specific grounding error. [H3–H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:93) · [July arithmetic and sourcing rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:5)\n    10\t\n    11\t### Thresholds and reading load\n    12\t\n    13\t- **60 of 73** is defensible as a coverage policy. It is **82.2%**, while the previous 80% rule would require **59** dates. State that 60 deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable dates. The two-sided bounds above should determine whether those missing dates matter.\n    14\t- **H1’s 5 points and 3 refusals** need an explicit tolerance rationale. With 266 linked judgements and zero control refusals, 13 linked refusals would still clear the gate. At this workload, the five-point rule already implies more than three additional refusals. **H5’s two extra failures** is a defensible tolerance for the fixed 14 question-dates per sequence once a single material error cannot supply the primary gain. Both are product tolerances to freeze before seeing results. [Thresholds](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:86) · [Workload basis](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:118)\n    15\t\n    16\tI counted **72 distinct v9/v14 answer pairs** on old-2’s 73 dates. Budget up to **438 pair classifications** across six sequences, about **213 linked v14 reviews** on the stored schedule, and **168 first and second H5 marks** before adjudication. The workload is feasible, but the protocol is incomplete. Before launch, pin a worksheet keyed by judgement IDs and date spans. Give readers the full forecast text, the endpoints and excerpt to record, and the exact request and dated sources for harm checks. Apply source-backed adjudication to verdict-changing disagreements in P, H3 and H4 as well as H5. The old-1 September 8 case, **$419B against $414–421B**, is a useful worked example of overlap. The roughly three-hour estimate covers the model run. Reader time needs its own estimate. [Hand-reading rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:68) · [Overlap example](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/parser-validation.txt:35) · [Run time](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:126)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
