{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md | sed -n '67,142p'; nl -ba DESIGN.md | sed -n '11,36p'\"","aggregated_output":"    67\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    68\t\n    69\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    70\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    71\tforce that day.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    74\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    75\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    76\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    77\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    78\t\n    79\t**Primary line, P, per pair n (round 24: three outcomes):** over all 73 dates, each arm's dates are definite (D), not definite\n    80\t(overlap or clear, N) or unresolved (U), each date keeping its own arm's class even where the other arm is unresolved. Linked-n's\n    81\tdefinite count lies between D_L and D_L + U_L; control-n's between D_C and D_C + U_C.\n    82\t- **Improved:** D_L + U_L < D_C (the least favourable completion still has fewer definite dates in the linked arm).\n    83\t- **Not improved:** D_L >= D_C + U_C (even the most favourable completion cannot give the linked arm fewer).\n    84\t- **Undecided:** otherwise (the unresolved dates could change the result).\n    85\t- **Testable:** D_C >= 2 (known definite dates of the control over all 73, not only dates readable in both arms).\n    86\t- **Readable:** each arm has at least 60 of the 73 dates resolved: a coverage policy that deliberately allows at most 13 unreadable\n    87\t  dates per arm (82.2%); the bounds above decide whether those dates matter.\n    88\t- **Credit (round 24):** a date the linked arm resolves as not definite while the control is definite on that date is a **gain**; every\n    89\t  distinct linked answer (v9's or v14's) that supplies a gain needed for \"improved\" is read for July's rule B (arithmetic following from\n    90\t  stated inputs; each assumption that sizes the result disclosed and supported). A gain resting on a material failure keeps its actual\n    91\t  class in the report but earns no credit: for P it counts definite. A gain that rests on an unsupported CPU increment (H4) is shown\n    92\t  with the increment removed when it can be quantified; when its size is unresolved and it could decide P, the pair is undecided.\n    93\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear.\n    94\t\n    95\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n    96\t- **H1, refusals (round 24):** on **matched reader-days** (a reader judged on that day in both arms of the pair), the attempted reader\n    97\t  judgements and the model refusals among them (first attempts the code refused, terminal refusals included); refusals on linked-only\n    98\t  propagation days reported apart. Harm when the linked arm has more than 3 refusals above its control on matched reader-days.\n    99\t  Rationale, frozen before the run: old-2's stored run refused 4 of 1,616 judgements (0.25%), about 0.6 expected per ~250 reader-days, so\n   100\t  3 extra is far above that base rate while one or two stays inside ordinary variation. A **linked double refusal** (the corrected answer\n   101\t  refused again: `judgement_refused_twice` stops the day) is a product failure: the verdict is **fail** whatever else holds; a control\n   102\t  double refusal makes the comparison invalid.\n   103\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n   104\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n   105\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n   106\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n   107\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n   108\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n   109\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. Checked regardless of keywords: every reader reference whose figure differs from\n   110\t  that reader's previous reference in the sequence in **amount, scope or attribution** (round 24: an unchanged $393B can move from a\n   111\t  company figure to a segment consensus), and every reader answer on the seven fixed dates below; keyword hits elsewhere are read too.\n   112\t  Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error and its control's conclusion in force on that date (its fresh judgement, or the one\n   113\t  carried forward) does not have the same specific error.\n   114\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   115\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. An unresolved CPU overlap that supplies a date's apparent\n   116\t  reconciliation (without the CPU-attributable uplift the date would be definite) counts that date definite in P.\n   117\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   118\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   119\t  source-backed read whose class decides. A material failure in a linked v9 or v14 answer whose carried-forward dates are counted\n   120\t  overlap or clear, and whose reconciliation rests on the failed assumption, counts those dates definite in P; a linked sequence with\n   121\t  more material failures than its control, by at least 2, is harm.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) a **linked double refusal** → fail; (2) **invalid or incomplete** (an infrastructure stop: budget, transport\n   124\tor harness; a control's double refusal; an H2 difference; an arm under 60 resolved dates) → inconclusive; (3) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3,\n   125\tH4 double count, H5) → fail; (4) **untestable** (a control with D_C < 2) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (5) **P**: all three\n   126\tpairs improved → pass; any pair not improved → fail; otherwise (some pair undecided) → inconclusive.\n   127\t\n   128\t**The worksheet (pinned before the launch; round 24):** built by `worksheet.py` from the six stores and archives after the run, its\n   129\tformat and code reviewed and run on the dry outputs before the send. One row per distinct v9/v14 answer pair per sequence, keyed by\n   130\tboth judgement ids and the pair's date span; it carries both answers' full `implied_outcome` and reasons, and fields for the reader:\n   131\tthe FY2027 low and high ends as each answer finally states them, the excerpt they come from, the class, a note. H3, H4 and H5 rows carry\n   132\tthe exact request text (dependency block marked), the read judgement each figure came through, and the dated source quotes. Worked\n   133\texample (old-1 09-08): Data Center \"$419 billion for FY2027\" against total revenue \"$414.3 billion ... supports roughly $414–421\n   134\tbillion\": low end 419 is not above high end 421, so **overlap**, though the parser took $414.3B and flagged it definite. Claude reads\n   135\tfirst; a blind reader (Codex, labels shuffled, key kept outside the repository) second; every disagreement that could change a pair's\n   136\toutcome, an H3 or H4 finding, or an H5 mark goes to a third, source-backed read whose class decides. Reading load on old-2's schedule:\n   137\tabout 72 distinct pairs a sequence (up to 438 classifications), about 213 linked v14 reviews, 168 H5 marks before adjudication;\n   138\testimated 6–10 hours of reading across the readers, apart from the run's three hours.\n   139\t\n   140\t**Reported beside, not gating:** each link's relation, direction and sign (the weak CPU link disclosed); self-made references\n   141\t(`reference_census.py`'s measures, the `unsourced` scan with the dependency block excluded); v14 linked against control; v21's implied\n   142\ttotal against v9 and v14; linked-only judgement days.\n    11\t## 1. The shape: old-2 continued with links\n    12\t\n    13\tEvery request the linking version builds byte-equal to a request old-2 sent is answered with old-2's answer (each sequence works on its\n    14\town copy of old-2's archive); only the requests the links change are bought: the link questions (Jev) and the judgements of a question\n    15\tthat reads a linked question. This is the July test's method (`../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md`)\n    16\tover the whole window.\n    17\t\n    18\tWhy this shape and not an independent forward run:\n    19\t- **Question 4 is answered by construction and checked by code.** No other request carries a judgement of another question:\n    20\t  the extraction request shows open questions with their takes lines (`placement.question_lines`, `placement.takes`: key and group\n    21\t  only); Jev's matching state and thesis text show the question, its ladder and its first two pieces of evidence\n    22\t  (`jev_matching.candidate_state`, `thesis_text`, `matching.describe_view`); a judgement shows its own evidence, its own previous\n    23\t  conclusion, the theme block and its dependencies (`judging.build_prompt`). Linking writes only asset-to-asset rows (D695), so a\n    24\t  changed judgement reaches only the questions that read it. The July test confirmed it: three days, 39 bought calls, every other\n    25\t  request answered from the archive. The code check: every judgement of a question outside the reading chains is equal, row for row,\n    26\t  to old-2's.\n    27\t- **The links' effect is not buried in sampling noise.** A fresh run re-samples 1,884 extractions and about 1,600 judgements; the\n    28\t  questions opened and their evidence would differ from old-2's for reasons that have nothing to do with links.\n    29\t- **Cost:** only the readers' judgements and the link questions are bought (section 6), not the ~$9 of a fresh run.\n    30\t\n    31\tWhat this shape cannot show, stated with the result (round 13): it is a comparison **conditional on old-2's questions and evidence**;\n    32\thow the link step behaves on question sets another draw would open (only old-2's openings are asked); a fresh run's cost and timing;\n    33\tthe idea routes (they read judged positions and reasons, `idea.py`, and the daily replay does not call them: outside this measurement).\n    34\tThe equality check compares nine projections (`rebuild_control.compare`), and unrelated judgements are compared by question and day,\n    35\tsince extra reader judgements shift row ids. The archive matches a request by its canonical identity (sorted keys), as production's\n    36\tarchive does.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md | sed -n '5,21p'; nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/round.py | sed -n '72,95p'; nl -ba parser-validation.txt | sed -n '33,38p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md | sed -n '5,21p'; nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/round.py | sed -n '72,95p'; nl -ba parser-validation.txt | sed -n '33,38p'\"","aggregated_output":"     5\t## Reading rule (frozen before the send; council R round 2, `codex-r2.md` §7(b), with round 3)\n     6\t- **A. Comparable answers:** both 07-12 answers give a FY2027 forecast in USD for their own quantity (a range by its stated ends);\n     7\t  quarters recorded where both give comparable figures; a missing annual figure leaves reconciliation untested.\n     8\t- **B. Arithmetic and sources:** reported figures keep their amount and scope; the arithmetic follows from the stated inputs within\n     9\t  rounding; each assumption that materially sizes the result is disclosed and supported by the material available that day; a linked\n    10\t  forecast is treated as a forecast with assumptions.\n    11\t- **C. Room for the rest:** the high end of the Data Center forecast is at least $6B below the low end of the company forecast (the Q1\n    12\t  floor: $81.6B total minus $75.25B Data Center); each comparable quarter keeps the segment within the total.\n    13\t- **D. The link did the work (linked arm):** matching chose \"v9 reads v14\", the writer committed it at seq 148, and v9 used v14's\n    14\t  forecast in its calculation or as a stated bound; delivery and use of the Bloomberg-compiled $393B (e195) recorded separately; a\n    15\t  mention or citation alone earns no credit.\n    16\t- **Pass:** at least two of three linked sequences meet A–D; at most one control meets A–C; no first-attempt refusal added and no new\n    17\t  material grounding error on either question across 07-10..07-12 (relabelling a system forecast as a market estimate, or treating\n    18\t  $393B as a Data Center consensus, is one). **Inconclusive:** two or more controls reconcile, fewer than two comparable sequences in\n    19\t  either arm, or a ceiling stop. **Fail:** a new material error, an added refusal, fewer than two correct link selections, or any other\n    20\t  miss. Failures take precedence.\n    21\t- **Readers:** Claude's marks written first, then a fresh blind Codex reader on the six sequences under shuffled labels, the key outside\n    72\t\n    73\tdef judge_day(conn, archive, cfg, day, seq, *, transport=None, log=print):\n    74\t    \"\"\"D63: one re-judging round per day over every open view whose reading changed that day, dependencies first,\n    75\t    recorded under the day's own processing record (input 'day:<date>'); a view already judged at this sequence\n    76\t    number (a resumed day) is not judged twice.\"\"\"\n    77\t    marker = {'input_id': f'day:{day}', 'publication_day': day}\n    78\t    changed = changed_views_of_day(conn, day)\n    79\t    result = RoundResult(marker['input_id'])\n    80\t    if not changed:\n    81\t        return result\n    82\t    try:\n    83\t        judged = judging.round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, sorted(changed), seq, marker, transport=transport)\n    84\t        result.judged = len(judged)\n    85\t        result.changed = sum(1 for j in judged if j['changed'])\n    86\t        result.cost += sum(j['cost'] for j in judged)\n    87\t        result.cached_calls += sum(1 for j in judged if j['cached'])\n    88\t        dispatch.mark(conn, marker, seq, 'judged', 'ok', f'{len(changed)} views changed, {len(judged)} judged, {result.changed} changed conclusions')\n    89\t        conn.commit()\n    90\t    except judging.JudgementRefused as error:\n    91\t        return _fail(conn, marker, seq, result, 'judged', 'judgement_refused_twice', 'judgement refused twice: ' + str(error), 'day')\n    92\t    except Exception as error:\n    93\t        return _fail(conn, marker, seq, result, 'judged', 'judging_failed', 'judging: ' + repr(error), type(error).__name__)\n    94\t    return result\n    95\t\n    33\t   DC end   : implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives a roughly $212 billion H2 baseline; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and about $418 billion for FY2027.\n    34\t   TOTAL end: ion Q3 guidance + approximately $128.5 billion for Q4, derived by carrying the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n    35\t2026-09-08 definite DC (419.0, 419.0, 'year') TOTAL (414.3, 414.3, 'year')\n    36\t   DC end   : owth into Q4 gives an H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and approximately $419 billion for FY2027.\n    37\t   TOTAL end: n Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414–421 billion.\n    38\t2026-09-09 definite DC (419.0, 419.0, 'leading') TOTAL (414.3, 414.3, 'year')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"**NOT READY.** Version 4 fixes the handling of unresolved dates and linked double refusals. The remaining blocker is how P gives or withdraws credit for a gain. Two readers can apply the current wording to the same forecasts and reach opposite verdicts. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n\n### Blocking changes\n\n1. **Review every credited gain in a provisionally improved pair.** “A gain needed for improved” leaves the review set open. If the control has two definite dates and the linked arm has zero, withdrawing either gain alone still leaves an improvement. Withdrawing both produces a tie. A reader who tests whether each gain is individually necessary could skip both. Require Rule B review of both supplying forecasts for every credited gain, deduplicated by judgement ID, then recompute P. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [July Rule B](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:8)\n2. **Use one rule for withdrawing credit.** H5 currently turns affected linked overlap or clear dates into definite dates, even where the control was also clear. A material error shared by both arms could therefore make linked P fail without any difference on those dates. Limit P adjustments to actual gains. For H4, say explicitly that removing a quantified unsupported CPU increment determines the **credit-adjusted** class. If the increment cannot be quantified and could decide the verdict, use the unresolved bounds. Keep the original forecast class, and keep confirmed double counting as harm. The fixed H5 sample still supplies its separate paired harm count. [Credit rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:88) · [H4 and H5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:114)\n\n### Round-24 findings that hold as fixed\n\n- **Bounds and precedence.** The two-sided bounds preserve known classes on all 73 dates. The round-24 example with 13 unresolved linked dates now returns undecided. The two-day floor uses known control dates, and **60 resolved dates per arm** is an explicit coverage policy. A linked double refusal now fails before the generic stop rule. H1 uses matched reader-days, so propagation-only days cannot dilute it. The written threshold tolerates **exactly three** excess refusals and fails at four. Align the rationale with that boundary. [Bounds and coverage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:79) · [H1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:95) · [Verdict precedence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:123)\n- **Sourcing and worksheet.** H3 now catches a changed scope or attribution at the same dollar amount and compares the specific error with the control conclusion in force that day. The worksheet specifies the judgement IDs, date spans, forecast excerpts, source traces, and adjudication. The $419B versus $414–421B example gives readers a concrete overlap case. In the dry review, count the second reader’s work separately from the number of distinct rows, and include H3 and the full gain review in the workload estimate. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:105) · [Worksheet](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:128)\n\nSettle the two credit rules before building. The harness review can then use the two examples above as $0 verdict checks."}}
